## What changed

`data.js` (lines 6–17) — the only file modified:

```js
function load() { return users; }
function isValidEmail(email) {
  return typeof email === "string" && email.includes("@");
}
function migrate() {
  return users.map((user, index) => ({
    id: index + 1,
    name: user.name,
    email: user.email,
    active: isValidEmail(user.email)
  }));
}
module.exports = { load, migrate };
```

- `migrate()` maps the legacy entries into fresh `{id, name, email, active}` objects, with `id` assigned `index + 1` (1-based, preserving order).
- `active` is `true` when the email contains `@`, `false` otherwise (e.g. `"no-mail"` → `false`).
- The source `users` array and its objects are never modified; `load()` still returns the original array as before.
- I added a small `isValidEmail` helper so the validation rule lives in one place.

